# Declarative Programming, and What It Means in SwiftUI

## 1. The general idea

Wikipedia's definition is the one worth memorising:

> **Declarative programming** is a programming paradigm that expresses the *logic* of a computation **without fully describing its control flow**. It describes *what* the program must accomplish, rather than *how* to accomplish it as a sequence of language primitives.

The contrast is with **imperative** programming, where you write the steps: do this, then that, then mutate this variable, then loop again.

You already use declarative tools every day:

| Declarative | The imperative alternative it replaces |
|---|---|
| `SELECT * FROM users WHERE age > 30` (SQL) | A loop that scans rows and builds a result set |
| `.button { color: red }` (CSS) | Manually painting pixels red |
| `[1, 2, 3].filter { $0 > 1 }` | A `for` loop with an accumulator array |
| A regex `^\d{3}-\d{4}$` | Character-by-character parsing code |

Two things are worth being precise about, because they're the source of most confusion:

1. **The "how" doesn't disappear — it moves.** SQL doesn't magically avoid work; a query planner turns your declaration into an execution plan. You gave up control of the *steps*, and in exchange you got a shorter, more reusable, more optimisable description.
2. **Declarative doesn't mean "no state."** It means state is *explicit and separated* from the description of the result. What's forbidden is hidden state that you mutate as a side effect of building the thing.

## 2. The same idea applied to UI

The classic framing: **UI = f(state)**.

Your view is a *function of your state*. You don't tell the screen "change that label's text." You say "for this value of `count`, the screen looks like this." When `count` changes, the framework re-runs your function and works out the minimal set of changes.

Here's the imperative version most UIKit code looks like:

```swift
final class CounterViewController: UIViewController {
    private let label = UILabel()
    private let button = UIButton(type: .system)
    private var count = 0

    override func viewDidLoad() {
        super.viewDidLoad()
        button.setTitle("Increment", for: .normal)
        button.addTarget(self, action: #selector(increment), for: .touchUpInside)
        // ...plus a dozen lines of Auto Layout constraints...
        updateLabel()               // 1. render once at startup
    }

    @objc private func increment() {
        count += 1                  // 2. mutate the model
        updateLabel()               // 3. YOU remember to push it into the UI
    }

    private func updateLabel() {
        label.text = "Count: \(count)"
    }
}
```

Note the failure modes baked into that shape: every new piece of state adds another `updateX()` call somebody has to remember to invoke. There are now *two* copies of the truth — `count` and `label.text` — and they can drift apart.

The declarative version:

```swift
struct CounterView: View {
    @State private var count = 0

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            Text("Count: \(count)")
            Button("Increment") { count += 1 }
        }
    }
}
```

`count += 1` is the only instruction in the file. Everything else is a *description* of what the screen looks like: a vertical stack containing a piece of text derived from `count`, and a button. There is no "update the label" step, because there is no second copy of the data to keep in sync.

## 3. Three properties that make SwiftUI declarative

**a) `body` is a description, not a script.** `body` is a computed property returning a *value*. It's a struct, not a live object on screen. SwiftUI calls it whenever it needs to know what the UI should look like — possibly many times, for reasons you don't control. That's why you must never put side effects in `body`.

**b) You describe *all* the states, not the transitions between them.** Imperative code encodes transitions ("on tap, hide the banner"). Declarative code encodes what each state *is*:

```swift
struct MessageView: View {
    let message: Message?

    var body: some View {
        if let message {
            Text(message.body)
                .padding()
                .background(.thinMaterial, in: RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 12))
        } else {
            Text("No messages")
                .foregroundStyle(.secondary)
        }
    }
}
```

You never wrote "show the banner" or "hide the banner." You declared that a message implies a bubble and no message implies a placeholder. SwiftUI handles appearing and disappearing, including animating between them.

**c) Derived values are computed, never stored.** The classic bug this kills:

```swift
struct ProfileEditor: View {
    @State private var firstName = ""
    @State private var lastName = ""

    // No @State here — it can't go stale, because it isn't stored.
    private var fullName: String {
        "\(firstName) \(lastName)".trimmingCharacters(in: .whitespaces)
    }

    var body: some View {
        Form {
            TextField("First name", text: $firstName)
            TextField("Last name", text: $lastName)
            Text("Hello, \(fullName.isEmpty ? "stranger" : fullName)!")
        }
    }
}
```

## 4. Collections: describe the mapping, not the bookkeeping

This is where declarative UI pays off most obviously.

```swift
struct Todo: Identifiable {
    let id = UUID()
    var title: String
}

struct TodoListView: View {
    @State private var todos = [
        Todo(title: "Learn SwiftUI"),
        Todo(title: "Ship something")
    ]

    var body: some View {
        List {
            ForEach(todos) { todo in
                Text(todo.title)
            }
            .onDelete { offsets in
                todos.remove(atOffsets: offsets)
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In UIKit terms you'd be calling `insertRows(at:with:)`, `deleteRows(at:with:)`, and `reloadData()`, keeping your array and the table view's internal state in agreement. Here you wrote one sentence — "for each todo, show its title" — and changed the array. SwiftUI compares the old array to the new one, figures out which rows appeared or vanished, and animates it.

The same shape handles filtering. Notice there's no separate "results" state to manage:

```swift
struct SearchView: View {
    @State private var query = ""
    let results: [Item]

    private var filtered: [Item] {
        query.isEmpty
            ? results
            : results.filter { $0.name.localizedCaseInsensitiveContains(query) }
    }

    var body: some View {
        List(filtered) { item in
            Text(item.name)
        }
        .searchable(text: $query, prompt: "Search")
        .overlay {
            if filtered.isEmpty {
                Text("No matches for “\(query)”")
                    .foregroundStyle(.secondary)
            }
        }
    }
}
```

## 5. `Binding`: two-way data flow without delegates

A `Binding` is a two-way reference to state that somebody else owns. It replaces target-action, delegate protocols, and KVO:

```swift
struct VolumeControl: View {
    @State private var level = 0.5

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            Text("Volume: \(Int(level * 100))%")
            VolumeSlider(level: $level)   // hand down a reference, not a value
        }
    }
}

struct VolumeSlider: View {
    @Binding var level: Double        // I don't own it; I read and write it

    var body: some View {
        Slider(value: $level, in: 0...1)
    }
}
```

The `$` prefix projects the `@State` into a `Binding`. When the slider moves, it writes straight into the parent's `level`, and the parent's `Text` re-renders. Nobody wrote a callback.

## 6. Composition: small declarations assembled into bigger ones

Declarative style scales by combining descriptions, the same way SQL subqueries do. SwiftUI's enabler is `@ViewBuilder`, a *result builder* — a Swift feature that lets you write a list of views inside a closure and have the compiler turn it into nested function calls:

```swift
struct CardView<Content: View>: View {
    @ViewBuilder let content: () -> Content

    var body: some View {
        VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 8) {
            content()
        }
        .padding()
        .background(.white, in: RoundedRectangle(cornerRadius: 12))
    }
}

// Usage: the trailing closure is a *list* of views, not a statement block.
CardView {
    Text("Title").font(.headline)
    Text("Subtitle").foregroundStyle(.secondary)
    Image(systemName: "star")
}
```

Behind the scenes the compiler rewrites that closure into something like `ViewBuilder.buildBlock(Text(...), Text(...), Image(...))` — the same trick that a list literal `[1, 2, 3]` uses. That's *why* SwiftUI's syntax reads like a description rather than a program: you're literally writing a data structure.

